<%# One application's CI, drawn as a testing card: the check meter on top, then the
    branch ladder as ONE PROGRESS BAR beneath it.

    THE METER is components/_ci_progress_meter, the same one the board card and the
    Next Release card already use — one mark per check inside a rail (✓ passed,
    ✗ failed, a spinner while running) with a clock that ticks live and freezes to the
    run's duration when it settles. Reusing it rather than drawing a second kind of
    meter is the point: the operator learns this vocabulary once, and a check here
    means what a check means everywhere else on the page.

    IT REPORTS ON ONE RUNG — Card#active_rung: the RELEASE rung when this repo is in
    the open candidate (so the card agrees with the tracker panel above it), otherwise
    a running suite if there is one, else the newest verdict. Three check-lists on one
    card is the two-rows-saying-the-same-thing shape the CI meter already collapsed.

    THE LADDER, beneath the meter, is ONE PROGRESS BAR in three segments reading
    `accepted → release → main` (drawn by tasks/_app_ladder_track, which the pinned
    strip shares). Two facts share each segment, and they do NOT share a channel:

      COLOUR — WHERE THE WORK IS.
        emerald  it moved through this rung (at `main`, it arrived)
        amber    it is sitting on this rung right now, and the count says how much
        faded    it has not got this far
        rose     CI FAILED here — the one thing loud enough to take the colour back
                 off progress, because a red rung must be actionable before anything
                 else moves rather than legible only as a small glyph.

      GLYPH  — WHAT CI SAID: ✓ green, spinner running, ✗ failed, and NOTHING when
               nothing was ingested. An absence must never wear the pass glyph.

    WHY THEY ARE SPLIT. This row's badges once spent their only colour on the CI
    verdict, so a card could say "release is green" but never "release is empty" — and
    green-and-empty is simply what `release` looks like between sweeps. One channel
    cannot carry both, which is why the row could not answer "where is this app in the
    devops process", the question it exists for.

    A segment reports CI's verdict and adds nothing to it. It used to also carry a
    `stale` state, inferred from stamp timestamps; that inference was wrong by
    construction and is gone — see Ci::LadderRung.

    AT REST — level, not in the open release, nothing failing — the card DIMS, drops
    its meter to a single line, and sorts last. It keeps the bar (three greens is the
    "everything arrived" picture) and the one fact that makes the silence legible: when
    this app last shipped. A dimmed card saying nothing at all is indistinguishable
    from a broken one.

    THE WHOLE CARD IS A LINK to the Actions run behind the active rung, so a click
    lands on the run in progress. When there is no run to open it degrades to a plain
    <div> — never a dead link, the same rule tasks/_release_phase_meter follows.

    Locals:
      card       - Ci::AppLadder::Card
      card_class - extra classes for the grid cell %>
<% card = local_assigns.fetch(:card) %>
<% attention = card.needs_attention? %>
<%# AT REST collapses the card: the CI meter and the sibling-lane row go, replaced by
    one quiet line. The header, the track and the review roll stay — two muted lines of
    board history are not what makes a card loud. Hover restores full opacity, so the
    dimming never costs the operator the ability to read a resting card. %>
<% at_rest = card.at_rest? %>
<% progress = at_rest ? nil : card.progress %>
<% run_url = card.run_url %>
<% linked = run_url.present? %>

<% card_classes = ["rounded-xl border bg-surface p-4 block min-w-0 transition-colors",
                   attention ? "border-amber-500/40" : "border-subtle",
                   at_rest ? "opacity-60 hover:opacity-100 focus-within:opacity-100" : nil,
                   local_assigns[:card_class]].compact.join(" ") %>
<% card_classes += " group hover:border-primary/50 focus-visible:outline focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-primary" if linked %>
<% card_options = {
     class: card_classes,
     data: { test: "app-ladder-card", repo: card.repo, attention: attention, linked: linked,
             position: card.position, at_rest: at_rest, furthest: card.furthest_rung },
     title: linked ? "#{card.repo} — open the GitHub Actions run for #{card.active_rung&.branch}" : card.repo
   } %>
<% if linked %>
  <% card_options.merge!(href: run_url, target: "_blank", rel: "noopener") %>
<% end %>

<%= content_tag(linked ? :a : :div, **card_options) do %>
  <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-2 mb-0.5 min-w-0">
    <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold truncate"><%= card.repo %></h3>
    <span class="flex items-center gap-1 shrink-0 text-[10px] uppercase tracking-wider text-muted">
      <%= card.gem? ? "gem" : "app" %>
      <%# The affordance: an arrow that surfaces on hover, so the card reads as
          clickable without a permanent chrome element competing with the meter. %>
      <% if linked %>
        <span class="opacity-0 transition-opacity group-hover:opacity-100" aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
      <% end %>
    </span>
  </div>

  <%# WHERE THIS APP IS IN THE DEVOPS PROCESS, in one word, under the name. The track
      below draws the same fact; this states it, so a glance down the row reads as a
      list of positions rather than five diagrams to decode. %>
  <div class="mb-2 text-[10px] font-medium <%= app_ladder_position_tone(card) %>"
       data-test="app-ladder-position"
       data-position="<%= card.position %>"
       title="<%= app_ladder_position_sentence(card) %>"><%= app_ladder_position_label(card) %></div>

  <%# THE QUIET LINE — the whole of a resting card's middle, standing where the meter
      would be.

      IT USED TO CARRY THE SHIP TOO ("nothing waiting · shipped 4d ago"), because the
      dimming needs a fact to be safe: "at rest" alone could mean "nothing is
      reporting". The ship now rides the MAIN MERGED line below, which EVERY card
      carries and which states the moment rather than the age — so keeping it here as
      well would put the same fact on one card twice, one line apart, at two
      precisions. The dimming is still safe: the fact simply moved one line down and
      got sharper. %>
  <% if at_rest %>
    <div class="mb-2.5 rounded border border-subtle bg-inset px-2 py-1.5 text-[10px] text-muted"
         data-test="app-ladder-at-rest">
      nothing waiting
    </div>
  <%# The CI meter. Rendered only when checks were actually ingested — an absent
      suite must not draw an empty rail that reads as "0 of 0 passed". %>
  <% elsif progress.present? %>
    <div class="mb-2.5" data-test="app-ladder-ci">
      <%# The label NAMES A LANE, not a generic "CI" — and on a multi-lane repo it names
          the one that is the NEWS (red, else running), because the meter now counts
          every lane rather than one. It used to disclose which single workflow was
          counted; that disclosure was what a green meter beside an amber pill needed,
          and widening the fold retired the need. %>
      <%= render "components/ci_progress_meter",
            progress: progress,
            label: [card.active_rung&.branch, card.meter_lane_label].compact.join(" · "),
            test_id: "app-ladder-ci-bar",
            clock: true %>
    </div>
  <% else %>
    <div class="mb-2.5 rounded border border-dashed border-subtle px-2 py-2 text-[10px] text-muted"
         data-test="app-ladder-ci-empty">
      no CI checks ingested for this app yet
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <%# THE LANE LEGEND. The pill and the meter now fold the SAME set — every suite lane
      on this branch and sha — so this row stopped being the apology it shipped as
      ("also on this commit", naming what the meter could not show) and became the key
      to a bar whose marks carry no lane identity. It is where a reader learns WHICH
      suite is the red one, and it is the only place that says so.
      Empty for every single-lane repo, where the meter's label already names it. %>
  <% others = at_rest ? [] : card.legend_lanes %>
  <% if others.any? %>
    <div class="mb-2.5 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-2 gap-y-1 text-[10px]"
         data-test="app-ladder-other-lanes">
      <span class="text-muted">lanes in this meter:</span>
      <% others.each do |lane| %>
        <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 <%= app_ladder_rung_tone(lane[:state])[:text] %>"
              data-test="app-ladder-other-lane"
              data-lane="<%= lane[:name] %>"
              data-state="<%= lane[:state] %>"
              title="<%= lane[:name] %> — <%= lane[:state] == :green ? "passed" : lane[:state] == :red ? "failed" : "running" %>; counted by the meter above">
          <span aria-hidden="true"><%= lane[:state] == :green ? "✓" : lane[:state] == :red ? "✗" : "○" %></span>
          <span><%= lane[:name] %></span>
        </span>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <%# THE LADDER, drawn as ONE PROGRESS BAR in three segments — extracted to
      tasks/_app_ladder_track so the pinned strip at the top of the page draws the
      card's own bar rather than a lookalike that could drift from it. Same shape the
      badge row had — three equal pills reading up the ladder — with the colour doing
      a different job: it now says WHERE THE WORK IS, and the glyph inside says what
      CI thought. %>
  <%= render "tasks/app_ladder_track", card: card %>

  <%# WHEN MAIN LAST TOOK CODE, under the rung that says it — the stamp, to the
      minute, for every card rather than only the resting ones.

      IT IS THE SHIP'S OWN CLOCK (Ci::AppLadder.shipped_index → the release's
      `shipped_at`), because on this ladder `release → main` IS the merge: `bin/release
      ship` fast-forwards it, and the release row records the moment production took
      the code. Nothing is derived twice — the at-rest line above reads the same field.

      ONE LINE, BOTH PRECISIONS. The label carries the AGE ("2d ago") and the value
      carries the MOMENT ("Aug 24 4:43a"), because the two answer different questions —
      "is this app going stale?" is read at a glance from the age, while "which ship was
      that?" is what an operator takes to the release log. Putting them on one line is
      what let the at-rest note above stop repeating the first of them.

      NEVER A GUESSED TIME. A repo whose last ship predates the scanned window renders
      an em dash and says so on hover; an invented stamp on a deploy board is worse
      than an admitted gap. %>
  <div class="mt-1.5 flex items-baseline justify-between gap-2 text-[10px] leading-none"
       data-test="app-ladder-main-merge"
       data-at="<%= card.last_shipped_at&.iso8601 %>"
       title="<%= app_ladder_main_merge_title(card) %>">
    <span class="truncate uppercase tracking-wide text-muted"
          data-test="app-ladder-main-merge-label"><%= app_ladder_main_merge_label(card) %></span>
    <span class="shrink-0 tabular-nums <%= card.last_shipped_at ? "text-secondary" : "text-muted" %>"
          data-test="app-ladder-main-merge-value"><%= app_ladder_main_merge_stamp(card) %></span>
  </div>

  <%# HOW LONG REVIEW TAKES on this app lately — the rolling ten (Review::DurationRoll).

      IT SITS DOWN HERE, below the ladder, ON PURPOSE. The CI meter at the top of this
      card already carries a minutes figure (the run clock — "accepted · ci  4m"), and
      two minute-values stacked together are two numbers one glance will merge. So this
      one is separated by the badges, wears no rail, and says the word "avg" out loud.
      It belongs with the parked counts anyway: both are derived from the task board
      rather than from CI, and both answer "how is this app's pipeline flowing", not
      "what is the suite doing right now".

      IT DEGRADES TO WORDS, NEVER TO A BLANK. A quiet repo — solana-studio had ZERO
      measured reviews on 2026-08-25, and mcritchie-industries' only one was excluded —
      renders "not enough data" plus the count that explains why. A card that renders
      blank or NaN for a quiet repo is worse than one that admits it has nothing.

      THE EXCLUDED COUNT IS NOT OPTIONAL. The 60-minute cut is what keeps interrupted
      reviews out of the average, so the card must say how many it dropped or the
      average is a claim with its evidence hidden. %>
  <%# KEPT ON A RESTING CARD, unlike the meter above. Two muted lines of board-derived
      flow history are not what makes a card loud, and a quiet board is precisely when
      "how long does review take on this app" is worth reading. %>
  <% roll = card.review %>
  <div class="mt-2.5 border-t border-subtle pt-2"
       data-test="app-ladder-review"
       data-repo="<%= card.repo %>"
       data-sample="<%= roll.sample %>"
       data-scanned="<%= roll.scanned %>"
       data-excluded="<%= roll.excluded %>"
       title="<%= app_ladder_review_title(roll) %>">
    <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-2 text-[10px] leading-none">
      <span class="font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-muted">review</span>
      <span class="shrink-0 tabular-nums font-medium <%= roll.any? ? "text-heading" : "text-muted" %>"
            data-test="app-ladder-review-value"><%= app_ladder_review_value(roll) %></span>
    </div>
    <div class="mt-1 text-right text-[10px] text-muted"
         data-test="app-ladder-review-note"><%= app_ladder_review_note(roll) %></div>
  </div>

  <%# THE PARKED FOOTNOTE IS GONE — folded into the track's node labels above.
      It said the same thing twice ("2 on release" under a `release` badge that could
      not show it), and as a footnote it was the smallest text on the card carrying the
      one fact the operator actually scans for. It now rides on the node it describes.
  %>
<% end %>
